    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the sum of all the positive divisors of 91, we first need to determine its prime factorization. We start by checking for divisibility by the smallest prime numbers.\n\n1. **Check for divisibility by 2:**\n   - 91 is odd, so it is not divisible by 2.\n\n2. **Check for divisibility by 3:**\n   - Sum of the digits of 91 is \\(9 + 1 = 10\\), which is not divisible by 3.\n   - Therefore, 91 is not divisible by 3.\n\n3. **Check for divisibility by 5:**\n   - 91 does not end in 0 or 5, so it is not divisible by 5.\n\n4. **Check for divisibility by 7:**\n   - Perform the division: \\(91 \\div 7 = 13\\).\n   - Since 91 divided by 7 equals 13 with no remainder, 91 is divisible by 7.\n   - Thus, \\(91 = 7 \\times 13\\).\n\nNext, we verify that 13 is a prime number. A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.\n\n- **Check for divisibility of 13 by prime numbers less than its square root (approximately 3.6):**\n  - 13 is not divisible by 2 (it is odd).\n  - 13 is not divisible by 3 (sum of digits, \\(1 + 3 = 4\\), is not divisible by 3).\n  - 13 is not divisible by 5 (it does not end in 0 or 5).\n\nSince 13 is not divisible by any prime number less than its square root, 13 is indeed a prime number.\n\nTherefore, the prime factorization of 91 is \\(91 = 7 \\times 13\\).\n\nTo find the sum of all positive divisors, we use the formula for the sum of divisors of a number based on its prime factorization. If \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\), then the sum of the divisors \\(D(n)\\) is given by:\n\\[\nD(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 91 = 7^1 \\times 13^1\\):\n\\[\nD(91) = (1 + 7)(1 + 13)\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n1 + 7 = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 13 = 14\n\\]\n\nNow multiply these results:\n\\[\nD(91) = 8 \\times 14 = 112\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the positive divisors of 91 is \\(\\boxed{112}\\).",
